    passage: "The Monroe Doctrine of 1823 stands as one of the most enduring principles of American foreign policy. In his annual message to Congress, President James Monroe articulated two core principles: first, that the American continents were no longer open to European colonization, and second, that the United States would not interfere in the internal affairs of European nations or in existing European colonies. The doctrine was a response to the threat of European intervention in the newly independent nations of Latin America. While the United States lacked the military power to enforce the doctrine at the time—the British Royal Navy, which controlled the Atlantic, was the real guarantor of the policy—the Monroe Doctrine became a cornerstone of American foreign policy. Over the following two centuries, it was invoked by various presidents to justify interventions in Latin America, expanded through the Roosevelt Corollary (which asserted the right of the United States to intervene in Latin American nations that were guilty of \"wrongdoing\"), and eventually incorporated into the charter of the Organization of American States.",
