    passage: "In recent years, the field of epigenetics has revolutionized our understanding of how genes and the environment interact. Epigenetics refers to changes in gene expression that do not involve alterations to the DNA sequence itself. Instead, chemical modifications—such as DNA methylation or histone modification—can turn genes on or off, effectively allowing cells to respond to environmental signals by changing which genes are active. What makes epigenetics particularly fascinating is that some epigenetic marks can be inherited: a parent's environmental experiences, including diet, stress, and exposure to toxins, can leave epigenetic marks that are passed to offspring. Studies in animals have shown that the effects of a traumatic experience can be transmitted across multiple generations through epigenetic mechanisms. However, researchers caution that the field is still young, and many claims about transgenerational epigenetic inheritance in humans remain controversial and require further investigation.",
