    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The rise of the novel as a literary form in eighteenth-century England is closely tied to the emergence of a new middle class with increased leisure time and disposable income. Unlike the epic poems and romances that preceded it, which were often set in distant mythological pasts and featured aristocratic heroes, the early novel focused on the experiences of ordinary people in contemporary settings. Works such as Daniel Defoe's \"Robinson Crusoe\" (1719) and Samuel Richardson's \"Pamela\" (1740) presented themselves as true accounts rather than fictions, a narrative strategy that appealed to readers' growing interest in individual psychology and the details of everyday life. The rise of circulating libraries, increased literacy rates among women, and the expansion of the publishing industry all contributed to the novel's popularity. By the end of the century, the novel had established itself as the dominant literary form—a position it has never surrendered.",
