    passage: "The identification of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) radiation in 1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson was a landmark discovery in cosmology. The CMB is a faint glow of microwave radiation that permeates the universe, a remnant of the Big Bang that occurred approximately 13.8 billion years ago. Penzias and Wilson were not looking for the CMB; they were trying to eliminate a persistent background noise that was interfering with their radio antenna experiments. After ruling out all possible sources of interference—including pigeon droppings inside the antenna—they concluded that the noise was coming from the universe itself. The discovery provided powerful confirmation of the Big Bang theory and effectively ended the debate between the Big Bang and the steady-state model of the universe, which had posited that the universe had no beginning and was continuously creating new matter. Penzias and Wilson were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1978 for their discovery.",
