    passage: "The philosophical debate between free will and determinism has occupied thinkers for millennia. Hard determinists argue that every event, including human decisions, is causally determined by prior events and the laws of nature, leaving no room for free will. Libertarians (in the philosophical sense) argue that humans possess genuine free will that is not determined by prior causes. Compatibilists attempt to reconcile these positions by redefining free will: they argue that an action is free if it is caused by the agent's own desires and intentions, even if those desires and intentions are themselves determined by prior causes. Recent research in neuroscience has added empirical dimensions to this debate. Experiments using brain imaging have shown that neural activity associated with a decision can be detected several seconds before a person becomes consciously aware of making that decision, suggesting that unconscious brain processes initiate actions before we consciously choose them.",
